 "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "4.\tThe upanishads contain many profound statements, but four are elevated above others as mahAvAkyas (“great statements”), one drawn from each of the four vEdas. Together, they encapsulate the entire teaching of vEdAnta in four lines. From the mAnDUkya upanishad of the atharva vEda comes “ayam AtmA brahma” (“this self is brahman”). From the aitarEya of the RgvEda comes “prajnAnam brahma” (“consciousness is brahman”). All four mahAvAkyas are interpreted to mean that the individual soul is identical to the ultimate universal consciousness, and they encapsulate the non-dual monism of advaita vEdAnta. X and Y are the two best-known of these. X comes from the brihadAraNyaka of the yajurvEda and makes this claim in the first person as a declaration. Y comes from the ChAndOgya of the sAmavEda and makes its claim in the second person, framing it as a father teaching his son this truth. ID X (10 points) and Y (10 points)", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-16T03:47:29Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Aham", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-16T03:47:30Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Aham B", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-16T03:47:31Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Brahmasmi", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-16T03:47:37Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Tat", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-16T03:47:39Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Tat tvamasi?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-16T03:47:40Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Tvam", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "